<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 273 0 R
/E (After determining that both three and four factor solutions were statistically viable, we examined the factor loadings to evaluate the interpretability and added informational value of each structure. We first inspected the loading matrix for the three-component PCF solution, followed by the matrix obtained when extracting four components. The comparison showed that the first three factors consistently captured meaningful and interpretable performance dimensions, each grouping together coherent sets of offensive, creative, and ball-progression metrics.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
558 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 273 0 R
/E (However, when introducing a fourth factor, we observed that this component contributed very little unique information. Its strongest loading, occurred only on the variable G-xG, while all remaining variables loaded more strongly onto one of the existing three components.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 273 0 R
/E (Because this fourth factor did not represent a distinct or interpretable performance dimension, and added minimal explanatory value, we concluded that retaining three principal components offered a more stable and meaningful factor structure for summarizing player performance.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
