983 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 306 0 R
/E (Injury Exclusion Bias: Still on the strict minutes threshold applied \(>35% of the season\), it prioritizes statistical stability but creates a blind spot regarding injured talent. Elite players who suffered long-term injuries during the 2024/2025 season were automatically excluded from the dataset. Consequently, the model fails to identify high-quality players whose market value may have dropped due to temporary unavailability, representing potential high-reward opportunities for medical departments willing to manage the risk.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
984 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 306 0 R
/E (Multidimensionality of Market Value: Our valuation model simplifies the concept of "Fair Price" primarily to a function of On-Field Performance and Age. However, real-world market value is driven by a broader set of variables not captured in this study, such as contract duration \(players with <1 year left are artificially cheaper\), media profile/commercial appeal, nationality premiums \(e.g., the "Homegrown" tax\), and the selling club's financial health. Therefore, a player identified as a "Hidden Bargain" might be cheap due to an expiring contract rather than a pure market inefficiency regarding their talent.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
985 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 306 0 R
/E (Positional Classification Bias: Our dataset construction relied strictly on the "Forward" \(FW\) classification provided by FBref. However, positional taxonomy in modern football is increasingly fluid. Many high-output offensive players, such as Raphinha, Florian Wirtz, or Cole Palmer, operate effectively as forwards but are often categorized as "Midfielders" \(MF\) or "Attacking Midfielders" within database taxonomies. Consequently, our analysis excludes these hybrid profiles, potentially missing elite offensive contributors who deliver "forward-like" statistical output but fall outside the rigid positional filter used for data collection.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
